          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What hair extensions are best for thin hair?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The best extension method depends on whether the hair is naturally thin or actively thinning. For clients whose hair has already become thinner over time and is no longer rapidly shedding, Keratin or Fusion Extensions can be a suitable option because the lightweight keratin bonds are attached to small sections of natural hair. Tape-In Extensions are also lightweight and can blend discreetly when professionally placed. Halo Extensions and Ponytail Extensions can add volume without placing individual bonds on the natural hair. A consultation is recommended before choosing a method."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What hair extensions are bad for thin hair?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Clip-in extensions are generally not recommended for thin hair because the natural hair may not be able to support the weight of the clips. Tight or heavy clips can pull on the roots and may contribute to breakage or traction alopecia. Microlink Extensions may be suitable for some clients with thin hair, but only a limited amount of volume can usually be added because too many strands may become visible or place unnecessary stress on the natural hair."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
